# URL redirects for Circulation Studio
# See DEPLOYMENT.md for detailed configuration.
#
# Cloudflare Pages reads this file from the output root. It reaches _site
# through the passthrough to public/, recorded in DESIGN_SYSTEM.md under Deploy
# pipeline. Plain passthrough copy silently lost it, so if a rule stops working
# check that _site/_redirects still exists before debugging the rule.
#
# FORMAT
#   <from>  <to>  <status>
#
# from    the old path, starting with /. May end in /* to match a subtree, and
#         a :placeholder captures one segment for reuse in <to>.
# to      the new path or a full URL.
# status  301 for a permanent move, which is what a migration wants. 302 only
#         for something genuinely temporary. 200 rewrites without changing the
#         address bar and is not what a migration wants.
#
# Rules are evaluated top to bottom and the FIRST match wins, so the specific
# ones go above the wildcards. A trailing /* rule placed too high swallows every
# specific rule underneath it.


# ============================================================
# MIGRATION MAP, NOT YET COLLECTED. Nothing below this line is live.
# ============================================================
#
# This is a rebuild of an existing indexed site, so the old URLs already have
# links and rankings pointing at them. Every one that does not land somewhere
# deliberate becomes a 404 that used to be a page.
#
# THE OLD URLS HAVE TO BE COLLECTED FROM THE LIVE SITE FIRST, and none are
# written here because inventing them would be worse than leaving it empty: a
# wrong rule looks exactly like a right one and nothing in the build can tell
# the difference. Collect them from the live circulationstudio.com, from Search
# Console's indexed pages, from the existing sitemap.xml, and from analytics for
# anything still receiving traffic. Then map each one by hand.
#
# THIS MUST BE DONE BEFORE THE DNS CUTOVER described in DEPLOYMENT.md, not
# after. Between cutover and the map landing, every old URL is a 404 being
# served to real visitors and to crawlers that will act on it.
#
# The new structure the map points into is two tiers, one flat segment each:
#
#   /yelp/<slug>/      Yelp Hub spokes
#   /library/<slug>/   everything else
#
# Worked example, in the form the real rules will take. Commented out because it
# is illustrative and the real slug is not known yet:
#
#   /blog/yelp-ads-cost  /yelp/what-yelp-ads-cost/  301
#
# reading: the old post at /blog/yelp-ads-cost moved permanently to the Yelp Hub
# spoke at /yelp/what-yelp-ads-cost/.
#
# A subtree whose pieces have no individual destination goes to the tier it
# belonged to rather than to the home page, because a reader who asked for one
# article is better served by the shelf it sat on than by the front door:
#
#   /blog/*  /library/  301
#
# That rule goes LAST once real rules exist, or it will swallow them.


# ============================================================
# LIVE RULES. Everything above this line is still commentary.
# ============================================================
#
# Both sources are live and indexed on the current site, confirmed against its
# sitemap.xml. Both destinations carry a trailing slash, because the build
# canonicalises to the slash form and Cloudflare 308s to it: a non-slash
# destination would make every one of these a two-hop redirect.
#
# The rank tracking rule is required rather than optional. The old site serves
# BOTH /yelp-rank-tracking-tool and /yelp-rank-tracking-landing-page, each
# self-canonicalising to itself, so the two have been splitting signals. The
# tool URL is the one that survives: its title is a real title, the other reads
# "Yelp Rank Tracking Landing Page", which is an internal label that leaked into
# a public one, and its copy is a launch announcement rather than a service
# page.
/yelp-rank-tracking-landing-page  /yelp-rank-tracking-tool/  301

# The accessibility statement kept the footer's URL rather than the live site's,
# so this is a rename. /accessibility is live and indexed today.
/accessibility  /accessibility-statement/  301
